# The web client is a single-page app served from a subdirectory, so a deep
# link like /app/chats/abc has no file behind it and must fall back to the
# app's own index.
#
# Firebase did this with `rewrites: {"source": "/app/**", "destination":
# "/app/index.html"}`. 200 is the Pages equivalent — a rewrite, not a redirect,
# so the URL the app reads from `location` is the one the user asked for.
#
# The marketing pages get no fallback on purpose: a typo under / should 404
# rather than silently render the landing page.
#
# Real files win over this pattern, so /app/assets/*.js is served as itself
# rather than as the app shell. That is Cloudflare's documented behaviour and
# has not been observed on this project yet — it is the first thing to check on
# the first deploy, because the failure mode is the app loading its own HTML
# where it expected JavaScript.
/app/* /app/index.html 200
